<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Ife</h1><p class="st-meta">1000 CE – 1900 CE (approx.) · capital Ile-Ife</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The sacred city of the Yoruba, cradle of their royal dynasties and home of some of Africa's finest naturalistic bronze and terracotta sculpture.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ile-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> was a substantial town by the end of the first millennium. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> tradition makes it the place where the world was created and where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oduduwa">Oduduwa</a>, ancestor of the Yoruba kings, established the first throne; his descendants are said to have founded the other Yoruba kingdoms and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oba-dynasty">Oba dynasty</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Ooni of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> ruled as a sacred king. Between the 12th and 15th centuries Ife artists cast <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a>-alloy heads and modelled terracottas of striking realism, and the city made glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> that were traded widely. Streets and courtyards were paved with potsherds. Ife's authority was chiefly religious, conferring legitimacy on other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> rulers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Political power passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a> and other states, and in the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ife">Ife</a> was devastated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> civil wars and long conflict with neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modakeke">Modakeke</a>. It came under British rule around 1900. The Ooni still reigns in Ile-Ife, one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria's</a> most senior traditional rulers.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-ife.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Ife" width="320" height="172"><figcaption>Image: Oramfe · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ile_Ife_aerial_scape.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1000 CE – 1900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ile-Ife</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Oduduwa, Obalufon II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Yoruba religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yoruba</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/nigeria">Nigeria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ile-Ife, Ife, Ooni of Ife kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1450&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ife" aria-label="Kingdom of Ife on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4806 1899 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Ife lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5126 2155L5109 2141M5126 2155L5094 2192"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5109 2141h0M5094 2192h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5126 2155h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5126 2155h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1450&amp;focus=kingdom-of-ife">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

